<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Sponheim</h1><p class="st-meta">1044 CE – 1437 CE (approx.) · capital Kreuznach</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Rhineland county between the Nahe and the Moselle, divided into two halves whose rulers died out in 1437, leaving the land to shared lordship.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts of Sponheim appear in 1044 near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahe">Nahe</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a>, with their castle at Sponheim and a family monastery founded there. A related branch became dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> from 1122. In the 13th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> county was divided into the Front County, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-kreuznach">Kreuznach</a>, and the Rear County, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/traben-trarbach">Trarbach</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled villages, vineyards and castles across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a> and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahe">Nahe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>, balancing between the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate</a>. The abbey of Sponheim later had the humanist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-trithemius">Johannes Trithemius</a> as abbot from 1483.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Countess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loretta-of-sponheim">Loretta of Sponheim</a> famously captured Archbishop Baldwin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> in 1328. The last ruler of the Front County, Countess Elisabeth, died in 1417 and the last of the Rear County, Count John V, in 1437. The lands passed to the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-veldenz">Veldenz</a>, then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Palatinate</a>, under shared rule until 1776.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-sponheim.jpg" alt="County of Sponheim" width="320" height="183"><figcaption>Image: Lencer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grafschaft_Sponheim.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1044 CE – 1437 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kreuznach</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Loretta of Sponheim, John V of Sponheim</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Moselle Franconian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sponheim, Front and Rear County of Sponheim</dd></dl></aside></div>
